This is the direct recursive version. At every mismatch it tries all three allowed operations and keeps the smallest result.

It is easy to follow, but it repeats many states. For example, the same suffix pair can be reached after different choices, so this version is mainly useful for understanding the recurrence, not for large inputs.

Edge cases are covered when either index reaches the end. If `start` is empty, the answer is the length of `target`, and the opposite case works the same way.

```javascript
class Solution {
    editDistance(start, target) {
        const solve = (i, j) => {
            if (i === start.length) {
                return target.length - j;
            }
            if (j === target.length) {
                return start.length - i;
            }

            if (start[i] === target[j]) {
                return solve(i + 1, j + 1);
            }

            const insert = solve(i, j + 1);
            const remove = solve(i + 1, j);
            const replace = solve(i + 1, j + 1);

            return 1 + Math.min(insert, remove, replace);
        };

        return solve(0, 0);
    }
}
```

Time complexity: exponential in the worst case because the same states are recalculated.
Space complexity: `O(m + n)` for the recursion stack.

The full table is not needed here. Each cell only uses the value above it, the value to its left, and the diagonal value. I keep the previous row and build the current row.

`previous[j]` represents the answer before adding the current character from `start`. After a row is complete, it becomes the previous row for the next iteration. The input strings are not changed.

For `start = "ab"` and `target = "ac"`, the first characters match. At the second position, replacing `b` with `c` is cheaper than inserting or deleting, so the result is `1`.

```cpp
class Solution {
  public:
    int editDistance(string start, string target) {
        int m = start.size();
        int n = target.size();

        vector<int> previous(n + 1);
        vector<int> current(n + 1);

        for (int j = 0; j <= n; j++) {
            previous[j] = j;
        }

        for (int i = 1; i <= m; i++) {
            current[0] = i;

            for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++) {
                if (start[i - 1] == target[j - 1]) {
                    current[j] = previous[j - 1];
                } else {
                    current[j] = 1 + min({
                        previous[j],     // delete
                        current[j - 1], // insert
                        previous[j - 1]  // replace
                    });
                }
            }

            previous.swap(current);
        }

        return previous[n];
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(m * n)`.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the two rows.

The answer for a pair of prefixes only depends on the previous row and the part of the current row already computed. This lets the code avoid storing the whole matrix.

The first row means converting an empty string into prefixes of `target`, so it contains insertion counts. For every new row, `current[0]` is the number of deletions needed to reach an empty target prefix. If the characters differ, the minimum of the three neighboring values is increased by one.

A useful small check is two equal strings. Every character takes the diagonal match branch, so the result stays `0`.

```csharp
class Solution
{
    public int editDistance(string start, string target)
    {
        int m = start.Length;
        int n = target.Length;
        int[] previous = new int[n + 1];
        int[] current = new int[n + 1];

        for (int j = 0; j <= n; j++)
        {
            previous[j] = j;
        }

        for (int i = 1; i <= m; i++)
        {
            current[0] = i;

            for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++)
            {
                if (start[i - 1] == target[j - 1])
                {
                    current[j] = previous[j - 1];
                }
                else
                {
                    int insert = current[j - 1] + 1;
                    int delete = previous[j] + 1;
                    int replace = previous[j - 1] + 1;
                    current[j] = Math.Min(insert, Math.Min(delete, replace));
                }
            }

            int[] temporary = previous;
            previous = current;
            current = temporary;
        }

        return previous[n];
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(m * n)`.
Space complexity: `O(n)`, since only two rows are stored.

This version starts from the beginning of both strings. The state `(i, j)` means that we still need to compare `start[i:]` with `target[j:]`.

If the characters are equal, both indexes move forward without a cost. For a mismatch, the three recursive calls represent insertion, deletion, and replacement. The dictionary prevents the same pair of indexes from being solved again.

The boundary cases are simple: if one string is finished, the remaining characters in the other string each need one operation. This avoids indexing past the end.

```python
class Solution:
    def editDistance(self, start, target):
        memo = {}

        def solve(i, j):
            if i == len(start):
                return len(target) - j
            if j == len(target):
                return len(start) - i

            if (i, j) in memo:
                return memo[(i, j)]

            if start[i] == target[j]:
                answer = solve(i + 1, j + 1)
            else:
                insert = solve(i, j + 1)
                delete = solve(i + 1, j)
                replace = solve(i + 1, j + 1)
                answer = 1 + min(insert, delete, replace)

            memo[(i, j)] = answer
            return answer

        return solve(0, 0)
```

Time complexity: `O(m * n)` because there are at most `m * n` saved states.
Space complexity: `O(m * n)` for the memo and up to `O(m + n)` recursion stack space.

I used a bottom-up table where `dp[i][j]` stores the minimum edits needed to change the first `i` characters of `start` into the first `j` characters of `target`.

An empty target needs `i` deletions, and an empty start needs `j` insertions. When the current characters match, the diagonal value is enough. Otherwise, the code checks insert, delete, and replace.

Dry run for `start = "cat"` and `target = "cut"`: the characters `c` and `c` match, `a` and `u` differ, and replacing `a` with `u` gives the final answer `1`. Empty strings are also handled by the first row and column.

```java
class Solution {
    public int editDistance(String start, String target) {
        int rows = start.length();
        int columns = target.length();
        int[][] dp = new int[rows + 1][columns + 1];

        for (int i = 0; i <= rows; i++) {
            dp[i][0] = i;
        }
        for (int j = 0; j <= columns; j++) {
            dp[0][j] = j;
        }

        for (int i = 1; i <= rows; i++) {
            for (int j = 1; j <= columns; j++) {
                if (start.charAt(i - 1) == target.charAt(j - 1)) {
                    dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j - 1];
                } else {
                    int insert = dp[i][j - 1];
                    int delete = dp[i - 1][j];
                    int replace = dp[i - 1][j - 1];
                    dp[i][j] = 1 + Math.min(insert, Math.min(delete, replace));
                }
            }
        }

        return dp[rows][columns];
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(m * n)`, where `m` and `n` are the string lengths.
Space complexity: `O(m * n)` for the table.

